review.detail:
The ZZZ control is not preserved as the work order states. routes.js:160 compares resolveTenantCurrency(existingTenant) against the event's spend currency, and resolveTenantCurrency falls back to 'INR' when canonicalCurrency returns null. So a tenant row holding an UNKNOWN code ('ZZZ') now silently ACCEPTS an INR spend event with 202, where on main that same event is a 400 CURRENCY_MISMATCH. The work order's own words are that 'an unknown-code tenant (ZZZ) still 400s with exactly today's message and details' and that 'only a mis-cased or padded tenant code changes from rejected to accepted' — that second claim is false. A row whose currency the build cannot name is now treated as INR on the write path, so bad data acquires a unit of account it never had. The read-side INR fallback is defensible (the renderer must draw something); the ingest side is different, because a 400 is the only signal an operator gets that the row is wrong, and this removes it. Nothing in the PR's tests covers this direction: test/api/events.test.js only pins ZZZ+USD (a 400, which still holds) and USD+EUR (a 400, which still holds). The ZZZ+INR case is untested in both directions, so CI is green over a behaviour change nobody claimed.

`src/api/routes.js:160`

Evidence: Measured on the PR branch (HEAD 5e9c77c), seeded DB, tenant_demo set to 'ZZZ' (sqlite3 UPDATE ... SELECT changes() -> 1):
  POST /v1/events {"event_id":"evt_zzz_inr","event_name":"spend.observed","currency":"INR","value":1000000000}
  -> status=202 {"receipt":"evt_zzz_inr","appended":true,"duplicate":false}
  -> GET /v1/metrics?tenant_id=tenant_demo = {spend_micros: 8200000000, qualified_volume: 3, qualified_cpl_micros: 2733333333}

Same event against unmodified main (origin/main ed5aa90), identical seed and identical 'ZZZ' UPDATE:
  -> status=400 {"code":"CURRENCY_MISMATCH","message":"tenant tenant_demo keeps ZZZ; spend in INR was rejected","details":{"tenant_currency":"ZZZ","received":"INR"}}
  -> GET /v1/metrics = {spend_micros: 0, qualified_volume: 3, qualified_cpl_micros: null}

Note the metrics half of this IS acknowledged by the work order (unknowns[]: "a 'ZZZ' tenant's GET /v1/metrics spend_micros changes from 0 on main to 7_200_000_000 after the fix, because the fallback is now applied consistently to the metric"; AC-3: "No metrics number is asserted at 'ZZZ'"). The INGEST half is not acknowledged anywhere, and AC-3's blanket sentence that a ZZZ tenant still 400s is what makes it read as a violation. The 8200000000 above (rather than 7200000000) is only because this QA run had already appended a USD row to the same tenant earlier in the session; the INR row is the delta.

Fix: Keep the comparison on the STORED string for the unknown-code case, and only canonicalise the mis-cased/padded case. Either resolve to null and branch:
  const tenantCurrency = canonicalCurrency(existingTenant?.currency);
  const mismatch = tenantCurrency === null
    ? existingTenant.currency !== spendCurrency   // unknown code: compare verbatim, as before
    : tenantCurrency !== spendCurrency;
  if (spendCurrency !== undefined && existingTenant && mismatch) { ... }
or, if the fallback-to-INR write behaviour is genuinely intended, state it in the work order as a deliberate widening of the write path and add the case to test/api/events.test.js (a 'ZZZ' tenant posting INR spend, with the expected status pinned) so the behaviour is asserted rather than incidental. The first option is smaller and restores main's semantics; the second is honest but changes what 'bad data' means at ingest. Given qa_script step 7 exists to prove 'the INR fallback was not weakened', the first is the one that keeps that step's intent.
